What is a Cluster?

A Cluster is the complete Kubernetes environment.

It contains:

Control Plane
+
Worker Nodes

Think of it as:

Company
=
Management Team
+
Employees
Control Plane (The Brain)

The Control Plane makes all decisions.

It does not run applications.

It manages applications.

Think of it as:
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CEO + Managers
1. API Server (The Reception Desk)

The API Server is the entry point to Kubernetes.

Whenever you run:

kubectl get pods

or

kubectl create deployment

the request goes to the API Server first.

Real-Life Example

Imagine entering a company.

You first talk to Reception.

You
 ↓
Reception
 ↓
Correct Department

Similarly:

kubectl
 ↓
API Server
 ↓
Kubernetes
---------------------------------------------------------------
2. ETCD (The Database)

ETCD stores all cluster information.

It stores:

Pods
Deployments
Services
Secrets
ConfigMaps
Node information
Real-Life Example

Think of ETCD as:

Company Database

Everything is stored there.

If ETCD is lost:

Cluster information is lost
-------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Scheduler (The Placement Team)

The Scheduler decides:

Which Node should run the Pod?

Example:

You create a Pod.

Pod Created
      ↓
Scheduler
      ↓
Select Node
Real-Life Example

A manager assigning employees to projects.

New Task
    ↓
Manager
    ↓
Employee Selected
----------------------------------------------------------------
4. Controller Manager (The Monitoring Team)

The Controller Manager constantly checks:

Desired State
vs
Actual State

Example:

You want:

3 Pods

But only:

2 Pods Running

Controller Manager notices:

Desired = 3
Current = 2

and creates another Pod.

Real-Life Example

A supervisor checking attendance.

Need 10 Employees
Only 9 Present
↓
Call One More Employee
Worker Node (The Actual Worker)

Nodes are machines where applications run.

A Node can be:

Virtual Machine
Physical Server
Cloud Instance

Example:

EC2 Instance
Oracle VM
VirtualBox VM
Components Inside a Node

Every Node contains:

Kubelet
Container Runtime
Pods
---------------------------------------------------------------------
5. Kubelet (The Local Employee)

Kubelet runs on every Node.

Responsibilities:

Talks to API Server
Creates Pods
Monitors Pod health
Real-Life Example

Think of Kubelet as:

Local Team Lead

The manager gives instructions.

The team lead executes them.
=--------------------------------------------------------------
6. Container Runtime (The Worker)

Container Runtime actually runs containers.

Examples:

containerd
CRI-O

In K3s:

containerd

is used by default.

Real-Life Example

Kubelet says:

Run nginx

Container Runtime says:

Okay, I'll start it.
--------------------------------------------------------------------
7. Pod (Smallest Deployable Unit)

A Pod contains one or more containers.

Example:

Pod
 └── nginx container

or

Pod
 ├── frontend container
 └── log collector container
Complete Flow of a Pod Creation

When you run:

kubectl run nginx --image=nginx

This happens:

kubectl
   ↓
API Server
   ↓
ETCD stores request
   ↓
Scheduler selects Node
   ↓
Kubelet receives instruction
   ↓
containerd pulls image
   ↓
Container starts
   ↓
Pod Running

This is the most important flow in Kubernetes architecture.

